-- Q20: SCHEDULE-20 explanation (1000 -> 2550 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$The way the scenario is framed implies that the project manager does not understand the basic principles behind agile estimation and planning. A story point is the typical unit of measure to estimate work items in the product backlog. The most important aspect to understand here is the relativity of the story point. The story point is a unit-less measure used in relative user story estimation techniques. Each team will have its own understanding of the size of the story point. Therefore, comparing story point estimates provided by two different teams would make no sense. The team's capacity is measured in story points. Capacity is the number of story points that a team completes in a predefined period of time, e.g., an iteration. Since each agile team interprets the size of the story point differently, each team would have its own capacity. This concept of each agile team having its own capacity is what the project manager most likely does not understand in the scenario provided. Details for Each Option: A Estimates in story points always look high. Incorrect. Answer choices with absolute statements, such as "always", "never", etc. typically represent incorrect answers. Saying that estimates expressed in story points 'always' look high would be inaccurate, to say the least. B Each agile team has its own capacity. Correct. A story point is a relative unit of measure. Each agile team would likely have its own understanding of how much work is involved in completing one story point. Hence, each agile team will have its own capacity and, therefore, cannot be compared with another team, regardless of how similar the projects the teams are working on may look like. C The team is using padding to secure its estimates. Incorrect. It is possible that the team is using padding to secure its estimates. However, comparing estimates provided by two different agile teams is a flawed approach since the size of a story point differs from one team to another, thus, implying that the capacity of one team would differ from the capacity of another team. D The two projects are not similar. Incorrect. There is not enough information in the scenario to determine if the projects are similar. However, this information is irrelevant. The point that the project manager is missing is that comparing the estimates provided by two different agile teams would not provide reliable results since each team is different, the size of a story point used by each team is different, and so the capacity of each team would differ from one another.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-20$sch22$;

-- Q21: SCHEDULE-21 explanation (1000 -> 2911 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$This question covers the topic of key performance indicators (KPIs), and specifically, lagging indicators. Lagging indicators measure project deliverables and provide information after the fact (which is why they are called "lagging"). Examples include the number of story points completed, schedule or cost variances, the amount of resources consumed, etc. In the scenario, the project manager uses key performance indicators to monitor the team's progress. Based on the measurements provided by one of the lagging indicators, the current sprint is trending to be completed earlier than planned. Various factors can contribute to this result. For example, if the number of story points completed exceeds the number of story points planned for the day on which the measurement was taken, one may reasonably assume the sprint is trending to be completed ahead of schedule. Therefore, of the choices provided, the number of completed story points exceeding the number of story points planned as of today was the most likely reason behind the project manager's conclusion about the sprint's early completion. Details for Each Option: A The lags on the critical path of the project schedule network diagram are shorter than its leads. Incorrect. While the critical path method is (or is not) used on this project, leads and lags are not considered indicators, whether lagging or not. They are the amount of time the successor can be advanced or delayed in respect to its predecessor. Regardless, there is no correlation between leads and lags, making this choice an incorrect answer. B The number of completed story points exceeds the number of story points planned as of today Correct. The number of story points completed compared to the number of story points planned is an example of a lagging indicator. When the team completes more story points than initially planned at a particular point midway into the sprint, the sprint is trending to be completed ahead of schedule, as indicated in the scenario. C The motivation of the team members in this sprint is higher than what it was in the previous one. Incorrect. Motivation can be a significant factor in a team's performance. Strong motivation may help improve performance, while weak motivation will likely hinder it. However, speculating on the sprint completion date based on motivation alone would be unwarranted. Besides, if at all, motivation is considered a leading rather than lagging indicator. D The length of the sprint has been reduced from seven (7) weeks to three and a half weeks (3.5). Incorrect. A typical sprint length is two weeks. A seven-week sprint is very uncommon. Even assuming this is the case in the scenario, sprint length is rarely adjusted midway into the project unless there are extraordinary circumstances. Besides, cutting the sprint length in half is likely to put the sprint completion date at risk rather than advance it.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-21$sch22$;

-- Q22: SCHEDULE-22 explanation (1000 -> 3148 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$In the scenario, the project operates in an uncertain and rapidly changing environment, and competitors releasing new products with features more advanced than those planned by the project. Such a project will often face a situation where the definition of a "good enough for release" goal may be subject to change. For example, if the project planned to release the product to the market within 12 months, but in the meanwhile, a competitor released a similar product with more advanced features, a decision of whether to release now with the current set of features or add features to be on par with the competitor and delay the release should be made. It is not uncommon that while the decision to add features and delay the release is made, another competitor releases an even more advanced product. When this happens, the performing organization will have to re-evaluate the situation and make the next decision accordingly. This can go on and on forever, which is why in the scenario, the project manager is concerned that the project is in danger due to "done drift". Therefore, of the choices provided, whether to release the product as-is or continue updating the features prior to launch is the decision that the project manager (and the performing organization) will have to make, making that choice the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A How to keep the team motivated despite more advanced features developed by competitors Incorrect. It is possible that in the uncertain and rapidly changing environment and stiff competition described by the scenario, the team's motivation may suffer. However, there is nothing in the question to suggest this is the case. Rather, the question focuses on the project manager's concern with "done drift", which is the moving target of completion. B When to switch from the adaptive to predictive development approach to prevent further changes Incorrect. The scenario implies the project is carried out using the adaptive development approach. In the uncertain and rapidly changing environment, switching from the adaptive to predictive approach to prevent further changes would do more harm than good to the project and even further hinder the project's ability to respond to market conditions. C What to do with the knowledge gained during the sprint retrospective meetings Incorrect. A sprint retrospective is a meeting where participants explore their work and results to improve both process and product. Nothing in the scenario suggests that there is uncertainty surrounding the knowledge gained during retrospectives. The knowledge gained at the sprint retrospective should be applied to future sprints, releases, and projects. D Whether to release the product as-is or continue updating the features prior to launch Correct. "Done drift" refers to the situation in which the project is forced to continuously change its objectives due to the frequently changing market conditions. When this happens, the main concern is whether to release the product as-is or continue updating the features prior to launch, making this choice the best answer to the question asked.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-22$sch22$;

-- Q23: SCHEDULE-23 explanation (1000 -> 3118 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$Little’s law is an important concept used to help project teams analyze and minimize work in progress (WIP) when implementing a Kanban board to manage workflow on an agile project. Little’s law is a mathematical proof developed by John D.C. Little that states that "the more things you have going at the same time, the longer each thing will take." To understand the formula, let's break down the different parts of it and describe what each variable means: Cycle Time = Work in progress / Throughput Cycle time is the time through the progress for each item. Work in progress (WIP) is the number of items the team works on at the same time. Throughput is the average time it takes the team to complete each item. Eventually, the cycle time represents the duration from the time the team starts working on a task until the team completes the task. From the scenario described, it seems as if the stakeholder used the formula to calculate the cycle time in the previous project as well and, after comparing the two, concluded that the cycle time in the current project is longer than the cycle time in the previous project. This information, however, is not required to answer the question correctly and is provided to make a realistic scenario. In general, it is possible and very likely that different projects will have different characteristics and KPIs. Cycle time is one of them. Note, cycle time is different from lead time. Lead time is the overall time that passes from the moment the work item is placed on the board until the item is delivered to the customer. Lead time is always longer than the cycle time because work items are typically waiting in the first column (e.g., the "To Do" column) before being pulled by a team member to be worked on. Then, after the item is done and ready to be delivered, it is possible that the item would wait in the "Done" column for various reasons before it's actually delivered to the customer. Details for Each Option: A Throughput divided by work in progress Incorrect. The numerator and denominator have been transposed in this choice, making it an incorrect answer. The correct formula to be applied to calculate the cycle time using Little's Law is work in progress divided by throughput. B Earned value divided by actual costs Incorrect. Earned value divided by actual costs results in the cost performance index (CPI), not cycle time. The CPI is typically used on traditional projects to measure the cost efficiency of budgeted resources. C Work in progress divided by throughput Correct. This answer choice represents the correct formula for cycle time used in Little's Law. The work in progress is the number of items the team works on at the same time, and the throughput is the average time it takes the team to complete each item. D The sum of estimates divided by three Incorrect. This answer choice loosely represents the formula for the triangular distribution used in three-point estimating as part of various traditional project management processes. The exact formula is the sum of most likely, optimistic, and pessimistic estimates divided by three.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-23$sch22$;

-- Q24: SCHEDULE-24 explanation (688 -> 2283 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$The velocity of the project team is a matter of trial and error for the first few iterations, after which it stabilizes and can then be used for estimation of work to be performed in each iteration for the remainder of the project. The scenario describes a project that will require the use of new technology to develop the product. This constraint makes the estimation of velocity extremely hard, if not impossible. Under these circumstances, requesting to run the first iteration without providing any estimation looks like the most reasonable approach. While this course of action is not always possible, of the choice provided, it's the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Use velocity of other teams in the organization who developed similar projects in the past Incorrect. Each team comprises different individuals, and team size varies from project to project. Additionally, the velocity of different teams cannot be compared because story point size is unique to each team. Therefore, relying on the estimates of another team would be misleading. B Provide a random number of story points and update relevant project artifacts with the estimate Incorrect. Updating the relevant project artifacts with estimates is proper project management practice. However, providing a random number of story points merely to keep the artifacts updated is not something a responsible team should do. C Commit to some number story points and, if unable to complete them, extend iteration length as needed Incorrect. Iteration length in agile projects is typically set at the beginning of the project and rarely (if at all) is changed. If the team is unable to complete the story points planned for the current iteration, the remaining story points are prioritized and moved to the next iteration. D Ask to complete the first iteration without estimation and estimate from the next iteration onward Correct. The scenario implies that there is no historical velocity available to use as a guideline for the first iteration. Under such circumstances, the team should ask the relevant stakeholders to complete the first iteration and use its velocity to estimate work for the next iteration onward. While not ideal, this choice is the best answer to the question asked.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-24$sch22$;

-- Q25: SCHEDULE-25 explanation (218 -> 1119 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$Key performance indicators are values that demonstrate how effectively a business objective is achieved. Cycle time is an indicator of how long it would take for the team to deliver a given amount of work. Details for Each Option: A WIP Incorrect. WIP is the amount of work in progress. WIP does not have a dimension of time and therefore would not allow the team to make reliable commitments about how long it takes for them to deliver work. B Defects Incorrect. Defect measures such as defect counts, defect rates, defect density, etc. measure quality, not time. Therefore, defects would not allow the team to make reliable commitments about how long it takes for them to deliver work. C Velocity Incorrect. Velocity is generally not used in Kanban. It shows how much work a team can complete in an iteration. Kanban does not have pre-defined iterations. D Cycle time Correct. Cycle time is a term borrowed from lean manufacturing and refers to the time it takes for a work item to go through part of an overall process. Cycle time allows teams to make commitments how long it would take for the team to deliver work.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-25$sch22$;

-- Q26: SCHEDULE-26 explanation (1000 -> 3076 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$Control limits are the thresholds that determine whether a process is in or out of control. The area within three standard deviations on either side of the control chart's centerline, or mean, is the normal distribution reflecting the expected data variation. A process is considered out of control when a data point exceeds a control limit. Control charts can be used to monitor various types of variables and, while typically used to monitor repetitive manufacturing tasks, they can be applied to other types of repetitive activities, such as agile iterations that are carried out using a pre-determined cadence and velocity. Velocity is an example of a variable that can be measured using the control charts. The scenario describes an agile team whose velocity in the last iteration fell below the control limit. However, that value is represented by only one data point. The team's ability to achieve their release goal depends on their performance across many iterations, so one data point below the lower limit may or may not affect their goal. The team members must try to do better in subsequent iterations. They should be as efficient as possible, limit their work in progress (WIP), and ultimately strive to do well on average. Up to that point, the team has been doing well with velocity somewhere around the mean. The mean represents the midway point of the upper and lower limit, which on average, is roughly the velocity required to achieve their goal. Details for Each Option: A Explain to the product owner that they cannot make their release date Incorrect. There is not enough information provided in the question to suggest that the team cannot achieve its goal. If the team members are able to complete more than average story points in consecutive iterations, the gap in velocity can be closed, and the release date met. B Try to make better-than-average progress in subsequent iterations Correct. While the agile approach promotes a sustainable work pace (which is one of the principles behind the Agile Manifesto), at times, as an exception, an extra effort might be required. If the team fails to meet the goal of one of the release iterations, the team members should do their best to try to close the gap and meet their original commitment. C Remove some of the low-value user stories from the release backlog Incorrect. The product (or release/project) backlog is established and maintained by the product owner in collaboration with the relevant stakeholders, including the project team members. Therefore, the decision on any changes to the backlog (regardless of the value of the user stories) cannot be made unilaterally by the team. D Increase the amount of work in progress (WIP) in the next iteration Incorrect. Work in progress (WIP) is the number of work items that are being worked on simultaneously by the team. WIP limits are typically set to make the workflow efficient. Increasing the WIP limits often leads to decreased productivity and throughput. Therefore, increasing WIP would likely have the opposite effect than the one desired.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-26$sch22$;

-- Q27: SCHEDULE-27 explanation (481 -> 1881 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$In hybrid environments, progress measurement must align with each methodology used. Predictive work is best tracked against baselines (start/finish dates, planned vs. actual effort), while adaptive work relies on metrics such as velocity, cycle time, lead time, and cumulative flow diagrams Combining these ensures executives see a complete, methodologically accurate picture of progress. This reflects PMBOK’s guidance to measure ongoing progress based on methodology Details for Each Option: A Compare actual start and finish dates against the predictive baseline while using velocity and cumulative flow diagrams for adaptive work to provide a holistic view. Correct: Combining these ensures executives see a complete, methodologically accurate picture of progress. This reflects PMBOK’s guidance to measure ongoing progress based on methodology. B Provide a high-level Gantt chart of all planned tasks with percent complete updates from team leads. Incorrect: Gantt chart with percent complete updates: Too simplistic; “percent complete” is subjective and often misleading. It does not reflect agile progress or predictive schedule variances effectively. C Present only earned value metrics (SPI and CPI) since they apply across both predictive and adaptive work. Incorrect: Only earned value metrics: Earned value is powerful in predictive projects but is not sufficient for adaptive work. Agile teams rely on working product increments, not cost/schedule indexes alone. D Report progress based on team retrospectives and qualitative feedback since adaptive teams cannot be measured quantitatively. Incorrect: Retrospectives and qualitative feedback only: While retrospectives are valuable, relying solely on qualitative measures ignores objective schedule tracking, making it impossible to accurately report against predictive milestones or forecast FDA submission readiness.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-27$sch22$;

-- Q28: SCHEDULE-28 explanation (732 -> 1651 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$Velocity is a measure of a team's rate of progress and is calculated by summing the number of story points assigned to each user story that the team completed during the iteration. If a team completed ten story points of work last iteration, our best guess is that they will complete ten story points this iteration. Because story points are estimates of relative size, this will be true whether they work on two five-point stories or five two-point stories. If we sum the story-point estimates for all desired features, we come up with a total size estimate for the project. If we know the team's velocity, we can divide size by velocity to arrive at an estimated number of iterations for the remainder of the project. Details for Each Option: A The number of user story points completed by the team per day Incorrect. Although story points are typically used in calculating velocity, the velocity is typically calculated based on the iteration rather than the day. B The number of user story points completed by the team per iteration Correct. Velocity is typically calculated as the sum of story points sizes for the features actually completed during an iteration. C The number of iterations completed by the team per release Incorrect. Measuring velocity per release means waiting for the release to be completed. Velocity should be calculated while the release is still in progress so that adjustments could be made to improve performance. D The number of story points planned to be completed in the next iteration Incorrect. Velocity is calculated based on the story points that have actually been completed rather than planned to be completed.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-28$sch22$;

-- Q29: SCHEDULE-29 explanation (535 -> 1518 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$The question implies that the Develop Schedule process is underway. One of the inputs to the Develop Schedule process is agreements. Agreements can take the form of a contract, memorandum of understanding (MOU), letters of agreement, verbal agreements, email, etc. Vendor agreements may already exist before the development of the schedule and may include vendor scheduling constraints. The project manager needs to take into account any vendor agreements that may affect the timeline when developing the project schedule. Details for Each Option: A Procurement management plan Incorrect. The procurement management plan is a component of the project management plan that describes how a project team will acquire goods and services from outside of the performing organization. However, the procurement management plan does not have details of vendor availability. B Project schedule Incorrect. The question suggests that the Develop Schedule process is being carried out. The project schedule is an output of the Develop Schedule process rather than an input. C Agreements Correct. Agreements with vendors may already exist before the development of the project schedule. It will be necessary for the project manager to understand any agreed-upon scheduling constraints before developing the schedule. D Schedule baseline Incorrect. It can be inferred from the question that the Develop Schedule process is being performed. The schedule baseline is one of the outputs of the Develop Schedule process and not an input.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-29$sch22$;

-- Q30: SCHEDULE-30 explanation (1000 -> 3090 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$When using a predictive model, the project's deliverables are typically deployed only at the end of the project rather than incrementally. Thus, with a pure predictive methodology, business value can only be realized once the project is completed. When agile methodologies are used, deliverables can be deployed iteratively and/or incrementally, which allows an organization to begin realizing business value even while the project is still underway. To answer this question correctly, one needs to understand the difference between response time, cycle time, and lead time. Response time represents the time a work item waits before work begins. Cycle time is measured from the time work begins until it is completed. Lead time is the total time it takes to deliver the work item to the customer. Thus lead time can be thought of as response time plus cycle time plus the time to deliver the completed work to the customer. The incorrect answer choices represent actions that might complete the work faster but do not include delivering the completed features to the customer sooner. Thus, of the choices provided, reducing the lead time is the only option that will allow the customer to realize business value sooner and while the project is still underway. Details for Each Option: A Improve cycle time by decreasing work in progress Incorrect. The cycle time is measured from the time work begins until it is completed. Reducing work in progress (WIP) may very well improve cycle time. Additionally, reducing WIP should reduce the risk of rework. If rework can be reduced or eliminated, the team may be able to complete work sooner. However, cycle time does not include the time it takes for completed features to be delivered to the customer. Thus, just improving cycle time will not necessarily allow the customer to begin realizing business value right away. B Request that the product owner reprioritize the product backlog Incorrect. Backlog prioritization is an ongoing activity that aligns the work items in the backlog with the customer value. While this activity may help deliver the most important features to the customer, merely reprioritizing the backlog is unlikely to result in the faster realization of the business value, as asked by the question. C Eliminate response time entirely from the development process Incorrect. Response time represents the time a work item waits before work begins. Response time is part of the overarching lead time; therefore, shortening the response time may address the customer request of realizing the business value sooner. However, eliminating the response time entirely seems unrealistic, making this answer incorrect. D Reduce lead time by delivering features once they are complete. Correct. Lead time is measured from the moment a work item is added to the Kanban board until it's delivered to the customer. Once the work item is delivered to the customer, its business value can start to be realized. Therefore, reducing lead time by delivering features once complete will permit the customer to realize business value sooner.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-30$sch22$;

-- Q31: SCHEDULE-31 explanation (1000 -> 3065 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$Earned value management (EVM) is typically associated with the predictive (traditional) project management approaches. However, applying EVM metrics to projects carried out using agile methods can be done similarly and serve as an effective tool to measure team performance. For example, the schedule performance index (SPI) is the number of story points (features) completed divided by the number of the story points (features) planned. The cost performance index (CPI) is the earned value (EV, which is the value of the completed story points or features) divided by the actual costs incurred during the period under measurement. The interpretation of the SPI and CPI values in agile projects are made similarly to the interpretation of those values in traditional projects. For example, an SPI less than 1.0 means the project (or iteration) is behind schedule, or, in other words, the team is working at a rate lower than planned. An SPI greater than 1.0 means the project (iteration) is ahead of schedule, i.e., the team is working at a rate higher than planned. A CPI less than 1.0 means the project (iteration) is over budget, or, in other words, the team earns less than a dollar on each dollar planned. A CPI greater than 1.0 indicates the project (iteration) is under budget, i.e., the team earns more than a dollar compared to each dollar planned. The question asks to determine the SPI. Based on the explanation above, the SPI = 60 / 80 = 0.750. In other words, the team worked at only 75% of the rate planned. Note, the reason the result is shown with 3 digits after the decimal place (0.750 instead of just 0.75) is to make sure that all answer choices are displayed consistently. Obviously, 0.750 is the same value as 0.75. Details for Each Option: A 1.333 Incorrect. This value, 1.333, could have resulted from the following calculation: 80 / 60 = 1.333. However, to calculate the SPI, one should divide the number of completed story points by the number of planned. In the scenario, the team planned 80 story points but only completed 60. Therefore, SPI = 60 / 80 = 0.750. B 0.750 Correct. In agile, the schedule performance index (SPI) can be calculated using the following formula: SPI = [number of completed story points] / [number of planned story points]. Based on the values provided in the scenario, SPI = 60 / 80 = 0.750. C 1.143 Incorrect. This value, 1.143, could have resulted from the following calculation: $80,000 / $70,000 = 1.333, where $80,000 is the planned value (PV) and $70,000 is the actual cost (AC). However, the SPI is calculated differently: SPI = 60 / 80 = 0.750, where 60 is the number of the completed story points and 80 is the number of those that were planned. D 0.857 Incorrect. This answer choice represents the cost performance indicator (CPI), which is the earned value (EV, the value of completed story points or features) divided by the actual costs (AC). The EV is 60 story points multiplied by $1,000, resulting in $60,000. The AC is given in the scenario: $70,000. Therefore, CPI = $60,000 / $70,000 = 0.857.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-31$sch22$;

-- Q32: SCHEDULE-32 explanation (667 -> 2290 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$Plan-driven managed projects can utilize some of the tools and techniques from agile methodology to support their project management. Incorporating agile methods with traditional plan-driven methods would make the overall project management approach hybrid. In the scenario described, the project team could use the agile technique of a spike. A spike is a short timeboxed effort established to address a risk, explore possible solutions, answer a question, or gather information, to name but a few common examples. While this may sound counter-intuitive, of the choices provided, addressing the audit as a spike is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Handle the audits as an unplanned spike Correct. Spikes are an agile technique used to address or better understand an unplanned circumstance or task. Spikes can also set aside project time to gather information, seek solutions, and/or conduct research. A spike can be a way for the project team to address a cybersecurity audit. B Reschedule the audits to the end of the project Incorrect. The timing of audits is not up to the project team. According to the scenario, audits can happen at any time and cannot be planned for. Consequently, the project manager and team do not have the power to shift the audits to the end of the project and, therefore, must handle the audits as they come up. C Request that the sponsor removes the audits Incorrect. Cybersecurity laws have been mandated by some authority or governance not mentioned in the question. Even though the issuer of the law is not mentioned, it is certainly implied that law is enforceable. The sponsor most likely cannot avoid the law, nor would it be advisable for them to do so. D Inform stakeholders that the project will miss its objectives Incorrect. Whether or not your project has the capacity to handle an audit, audits per the cybersecurity law must be permitted. Consequently, the project manager and team have to find ways to handle the audits. This may require other tasks to be delayed or mitigated. Communicating with stakeholders on the impacts of the audits is important, but making the assumption that the project will miss its objectives without first evaluating the impact of the audit on all project constraints is premature.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-32$sch22$;

-- Q33: SCHEDULE-33 explanation (927 -> 2028 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $sch22$A release burndown chart shows the number of story points remaining to be completed in the release. When the curve representing the actual completed work is above the straight descending line of the projected work, the team completed less work than originally planned and therefore is behind schedule. This is the only conclusion that can be drawn at this point. One can argue that based on the velocity of 20 story points per iteration and the gap of 40 story points the work will be finished four weeks later than originally planned (assuming the iteration length is two weeks). However, there are no exact numbers provided by the question. Rather, the question uses language such as “about two months” and “approximately 40 points”, meaning an accurate estimation for the work to be completed cannot be provided. The team is in the middle of the release and cannot predict yet what could happen towards its end. Details for Each Option: A The team is ahead of schedule. Incorrect. In a release burndown chart, if the actual work is above the projected one, then the team is behind schedule. B The team is behind schedule and work will be finished two weeks later than originally planned. Incorrect. Although the team is behind schedule in this scenario, it cannot be determined, based on the vagueness of the question, how much longer it will take to complete the remaining iterations. Additionally, even with the vague information provided by the question, it will likely take the team more than an additional two weeks to complete. C The team is behind schedule and work will be finished four weeks later than originally planned. Incorrect. Although the team is behind schedule in this scenario, it cannot be determined, based on the vagueness of the question, how much longer it will take to complete the remaining iterations. D The team is behind schedule. Correct. With only approximations for the completed work, the only conclusion that can be definitively drawn, in this scenario, is that the team is behind schedule.$sch22$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $sch22$SCHEDULE-33$sch22$;
